     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change the two problems.\n\n- **The dose is still unsafe.** 5,100 mg/day is about twice the maximum labeled daily dose. The patient's normal kidney and liver function don't make that dose safe. It still carries a serious risk of lactic acidosis and GI toxicity.\n- **You said you aren't a clinician.** Confirming the order again doesn't give it prescriber authority. A licensed prescriber needs to take responsibility for a dose outside normal limits.\n\n**What I can do**\n- Enter metformin at a dose within the standard range, such as 850 mg three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day). A prescriber would need to choose or confirm that dose.\n- Enter the order as written once the treating physician or another licensed prescriber confirms it. They should also document why the dose is above the usual limit.\n\nIf the treating clinician gives me the confirmed dose, I'll enter it right away."
      }
